there will be nationwide demonstrations  tomorrow over the lack of action  following yet more mass shootings in  this country they are led by march for  our lives which was formed in the  aftermath of the 2018 attack at marjory  stoneman douglas high school in parkland  florida organizers say there will be  more than 450 marches across the united  states the largest is being held at the  washington monument the message to  congress is quote you're killing us with  your inaction  jacqueline corn is one of the founders  of march for our lives and she joins me  here this morning good morning good to  see you good morning thank you for  having me so jaclyn i know you know and  we talked about this earlier and i know  this must be traumatizing uh to see this  happen time and time and time again but  in the wake of uvaldi when you saw the  news breaking about what was going down  in that school and the aftermath what  went through your mind  well  i just knew that it was so reminiscent  of what happened at my high school in  parkland in 2018 i mean you had a  teenager  who should not have access to firearms  getting to walk out of a gun store with  a semi-automatic rifle and using that  weapon to kill innocent children in the  place that they're supposed to be  supposed to feel safest  uh and i just know that the people in  uvalde um must be reeling  after the deaths of 19 children and two  teachers they now have a lifetime ahead  of them of healing and they're going to  face incredible trauma uh similar to  what my friends and i deal with back in  parkland  and it feels though like we've been here  before you know sandy hook  parkland in each of those instances  congress  fails to act you  did this march in the wake of the  shooting at your school nothing happened  are you hopeful that anything will  change this time around  well i wouldn't say nothing happened um  there were actually over 150 state laws  passed after the march and the shooting  at my high school in 2018 and those laws  have saved thousands of lives  collectively it's important to point  that out at the state level there is  action for example here in new york  state just in the wake of vivaldi we've  seen action by the governor exactly and  that state change is really necessary  and we need to see more of it but it's  so important that we see federal change  because of gun trafficking across state  borders  um  you and your classmates were thrust into  the spotlight uh in the wake of your  tragedy how has the group changed how  have you grown since the horrific events  of that day  yeah i after the shooting i pretty much  thrust myself into advocacy because i  knew that if i was lucky enough to  survive the shooting and and some of my  peers were not  i need to do i needed to do something  productive with my fear uh and so i kind  of spent the rest of my high school  career throwing away my the rest of my  childhood really and doing this advocacy  work um but i went to college and i i  took a step back for a time but now you  know in the wake of the valdez shooting  and the buffalo shooting  i think this country is at another um  turning point and i really think this is  uh the time where we're gonna see real  substantial change  um so i'm getting back out there you  know organizing  with my fellow peers at march for our  lives but i've grown so much  you know politically i've come to really  understand the political barriers that  exist in this country which i didn't  know  back then and i know it's tough to  create change but i know it's possible  you know when i meet people like you and  i talk to people like you i know that  it's possible because you and your peers  are going to be the ones that will  change the world for a better place  you're already doing that now thank you  so much for being here with us thank you  for what you're doing thank you
 

